  <h2 id="canada">Canada: PGWP, and where the biotech jobs actually sit</h2>

  <p>
    Canada's pitch is genuinely different from the US: the work-permit window is longer relative to degree length, and the government has been unusually transparent about the rules recently.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-box">
    <p class="callout-box__label">The rule most people miss</p>
    <p>
      Ottawa confirmed in January 2026 that the PGWP-eligible field-of-study list will not change for the entirety of 2026 — a deliberate freeze meant to give students planning certainty. More importantly for a biotech master's specifically: degree holders (bachelor's, master's, and PhD) are exempt from the field-of-study eligibility list altogether. That restriction only applies to diploma and certificate-level programs, which is a genuinely different mechanic from the US's STEM-designation requirement or Australia's shortage-list gate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the PGWP actually gives you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Up to three years of open work authorisation for a program of two years or more, usable with any employer.</li>
        <li>No job offer required to obtain it, unlike some employer-sponsored routes.</li>
        <li>Degree holders bypass the field-of-study restriction entirely.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What it does not give you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The PGWP itself is not permanent residency. You still need Express Entry Comprehensive Ranking System points or a provincial nominee stream afterward.</li>
        <li>Biotech-specific provincial nominee streams are limited and competitive, not a rubber stamp.</li>
        <li>A PGWP that runs out without PR progress does not extend itself automatically.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

